# 🌺 English Contemplation

## **Perhaps Bondage Is in the Grip**

Vedānta begins with what appears to be a cosmic question:

**How can a changing universe arise from changeless Brahman?**

But eventually the inquiry becomes intimate:

**How did the changeless Self come to regard itself as a changing person?**

Perhaps it never did.

A body appeared.
A mind appeared.
Relationships appeared.
Experiences appeared.

Then identification added two powerful words:

**I.
Mine.**

What changes became “I.”

What surrounds what changes became “mine.”

And from there, every alteration in circumstance began to feel like an alteration in Being itself.

The parable of the monkey is therefore profound.

The monkey says, “The jar has trapped me.”

But the jar is not holding the monkey.

The monkey is holding the grain.

Freedom requires no destruction of the jar.

Only the opening of the fist.

Perhaps spiritual freedom is similar.

The world need not disappear.

Life need not stop.

Love need not diminish.

What falls away is the demand that something impermanent must make the Self permanent, complete, or secure.

Then another discovery becomes possible:

**Our connection with the changing was never permanent,
and our connection with Reality was never broken.**

Yoga, at its deepest, may therefore be less a new union than the ending of an imagined separation.

